To address this challenge, we developed an ISO 26262 Work Product Generator, an internal web app  designed to support the early stages of the Functional Safety lifecycle.
 
Following the ISO 26262 concept phase, our solution focuses on two key work products: Item Definition and Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment, or HARA, including the derivation of Safety Goals.
 
The generation workflow begins with OEM-provided system architecture and system concept specifications. These inputs are processed in accordance with OEM-defined ISO 26262 work product templates to derive the Item Definition and subsequent HARA work products, maintaining alignment with the prescribed Functional Safety development process.
 
Qwen AI model assists in drafting each section, while a Functional Safety engineer reviews and approves the content before proceeding to the next stage.
Importantly, safety-related assessments such as Exposure, Severity, Controllability and ASIL determination are calculated through a dedicated rule-based engine, following the applicable HARA guidelines and ISO 26262 Part 3.
The solution remains within the company network, and all generated outputs are treated as drafts for expert review and approval.
In this way, we are not changing the ISO 26262 process; we are making its execution more efficient through AI-assisted engineering.
